import "std/regexp"
import "std/text"

# A scan whose PEAK grows with the subject, which no other benchmark has.
# escapebench measures storage the arena never held; this measures storage the
# arena holds and never rewinds. From outside the process the two look the
# same, and inside they have nothing in common.
#
# The shape is prose_check's, which is the real workload behind it: walk a
# subject once, matching at every position, keeping nothing. Peak should be
# flat in the subject length, because a position that matched nothing has no
# result to hold. It is not flat, and nothing sweeps per position: every
# position's scratch survives to the end.
#
# THIS HEADER USED TO SAY the index is seeded at TOP, TOP carries the BYTES
# bit, and the beat guard refuses to rewind a cluster whose parameter might be
# bytes. The guard is real; the measurement below puts the cause elsewhere.
#
# regexp/walked/5 is the loop that keeps the scratch. The beat report gives it
# two reasons to decline -- "another group tail-calls it (unbracketed entry)"
# and "argument 1 also carries heap". Measured on 2026-09-08, each against this
# benchmark's 198,180,864 bytes over 189 blocks: removing the BYTES condition
# from all three guard sites leaves it at 198,180,864 over 189, to the byte;
# disabling the unbracketed-entry check leaves it there too; clearing BOTH, so
# the group classifies as a carry beat evacuating arguments 1, 3, 4 and 5,
# leaves the emitted IR byte-identical to baseline.
#
# What decides it sits downstream of the classifier. codegen reads beat_loops,
# and beat_loops drops every group whose file begins "std/" or "lib/" from the
# carry tier, so regexp/walked's carry is stripped and its id dropped whatever
# the verdict was. Clear that filter as well and the peak is 1,048,576 bytes
# over one block, with alloc_bytes unchanged at 197,577,484: the same
# allocation, now reclaimed.
#
# The filter earns its place. Removing it wholesale left runbench still running
# after ten minutes against a 0.4-second baseline, because every library loop
# begins evacuating, and the 2026-09-01 sitting priced the same removal at
# -0.56 welfare under the objective of that day. This row is the price of the
# carry tier being decided by a path prefix rather than by the property the
# prefix stands in for, and it moves when that is fixed.
#
# The pattern retains nothing on purpose. A pattern that matched would make
# peak grow for an honest reason — the matches are real data — and the row
# would then move whenever the generator changed rather than when the compiler
# did. `zzq` never appears in the alphabet the subject is built from.
pattern = "[a-z]+zzq"

alphabet = "abcdefghijklm"

# Doubling, so building an n-byte subject costs a logarithmic number of
# allocations and about 2n bytes at peak. A per-character loop would hold n
# pieces at once and put an honest O(n) term in the row this benchmark exists
# to keep free of them.
fn doubled s n
  wider s n (length s < n)

fn wider s _ false
  s

fn wider s n true
  doubled (text/join [s s] "") n

pub fn total n
  subject = text/slice (doubled alphabet n) 1 n
  length (regexp/find_all pattern subject)
